  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R30", "R47"],
      "pins": ["U7.8"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "GND"],
      "observed": "The TPS62933 buck (U7) feedback divider is drawn with R30 = 24.3 kΩ (output to FB) and R47 = 10 kΩ (FB to GND), a ratio of (1 + 24.3/10) = 3.43. With the TPS62933's 0.8 V feedback reference this sets the switching regulator to about 2.7 V, not 5 V.",
      "expected": "For the specified 24 V → 5 V rail the divider must be about 52.3 kΩ / 10 kΩ (or equivalently 24.3 kΩ / 4.64 kΩ); the drawn values are off by roughly a factor of two and give the wrong output voltage.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "24V->5V 3A BUCK ... U7 TPS62933 ... R30 24.3k ... R47 10k ... FB 8 ... +5V (Power Train sheet)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U1 — TPS62933 (TI) | 24 V → 5 V synchronous buck regulator, 3 A ... SW node feeds inductor L1 into the board's main +5V rail, which powers the ESP32 DevKit's 5V0 pin and the downstream LDO."}
      ],
      "impact": "The main +5V rail would regulate to roughly 2.7 V instead of 5 V. The ESP32-C6 DevKit (5V0 pin) and the AZ1117-3.3 LDO (which needs 5 V headroom to produce 3.3 V) would be under-powered, so the controller would not boot and the whole board would be non-functional.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design is largely consistent with the specification, but the TPS62933 buck feedback divider (R30 = 24.3 kΩ, R47 = 10 kΩ) is drawn with a ratio that produces about 2.7 V instead of the specified 5 V rail, which would prevent the board from operating; this is the most likely injected deviation."
}

